body:
The problem is, the way the current implementation works, it's not possible for the shader to discern between flickering and ghosting.

Say the shader reads a rendered value that is completely unlike the history, do we:
- A: assume the value is flicker, mostly ignore it, and use primarily our history value
- B: assume the value is correct, and that the values in the history are invalid and ghosted, and use primarily our rendered value

Doing A all the times inevitably leads to ghosting. Doing B all the times inevitably leads to jittering.

Something else is needed to inform the choice, since there's not enough information to work with as things are right now.

This is a very basic, naive version of TAA:

```glsl
vec3 s = textureLod(color_buffer, uv, 0.0).rgb;
float depth = textureLod(depth_buffer, uv, 0.0).r;
vec3 history = sample_catmull_rom_9(history_buffer, uv_reprojected, params.resolution).rgb;
if (depth > 0 && all(lessThan(uv_reprojected, vec2(1.0))) && all(greaterThan(uv_reprojected, vec2(0.0)))) {
	s = mix(s, history, 0.90);
}
```

This is how it behaves:

https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7fb311be-4c14-4a6a-8759-ca54cf444b80

Of course the ghosting is extreme, but there is no flickering, and the visual quality is otherwise really good, with even small details like the thin antennas rendering correctly.

The code above has a very basic check, which on sky samples (which have `depth == 0`) completely rejects history. Even just this basic check makes a huge difference when moving the cube in the sky, as can be seen in the video.

Feels to me like maybe defaulting to very aggressive history blending, which maintains high quality and prevents flickering, but working on clever strategies to remove ghosting, might be the best way forward?
